# Terraform
**/.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfplan
*.tfbackend
tfplan
crash.log

# The human-readable half of a saved plan. The binary is anchored above, but
# the review file carries the same disclosure — the complete delta, including
# the home IP inside NSG rules and the hub public IP — which is exactly why CI
# writes it to the private tfplans container instead of a log. The runbook
# then tells the operator to download it into their working tree to review it
# before approving a hash, and that tree is a clone of a PUBLIC repository.
plan-output.txt
destroy-output.txt
*-plan-output.txt
*-plan-summary.txt
*-plan-manifest.json
destroy-summary.txt
destroy-manifest.json
changed-resources.txt

# Secrets & local config — NEVER commit
*.tfvars
!*.tfvars.example
.env
*.pem
*.key
wg0.conf

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
dist/
*.egg-info/

# OS
.DS_Store

# graphify knowledge-graph output (local tooling artifact)
graphify-out/

# SDD scratch workspaces
.superpowers/

# Local, uncommitted credentials for operator runs (never commit)
.env.local
*.env.local

# SpatiumDDI working clone — the DDI control plane this lab reconciles against.
# A separate upstream project, kept here only so the reconciler can be developed
# against a running instance.
#
# Anchored with a leading slash: this rule is meant for the clone at the repo
# root and nothing else. Unanchored, it would also swallow any future
# ddi-reconciler/src/ddi_reconciler/spatiumddi/ package directory, which would
# be silently untracked — the worst kind of ignore bug.
#
# It carries its own .git, so `git add -A` would record a gitlink (an unusable
# half-submodule with no .gitmodules entry), not the clone's contents. The
# reason to ignore it is what sits inside the working tree: local config and
# .env files for the running instance, which have no business here.
/spatiumddi/
